EXT. SANDBAR - ESTUARY FERRY - DAY

A small vehicle ferry grounded on a mile of shining sandbar. The tide has gone out and left her heeled over, her ramp resting on mud. Gulls stalk the wet. Heat shimmers on the water's far edge.

CAPTAIN MIRIAM OKAFOR-LINDQVIST, fifties, sits on the ramp, her cap beside her. DENNIS ABERNATHY, forties, a salesman in a ruined suit, stands at the rail holding his shoes. FATIMA HADDAD, thirties, a nurse, kneels beside a child asleep on a coat. CHILD'S FATHER, TOMASZ NOWAK, thirties, sits by her with his hand on the child's back. OLD PETRONELLA, eighties, sits on a bollard knitting. JASPER, twenties, the engineer, stands at the engine hatch, wiping his hands on a rag.

Miriam checks her watch. She checks it again. She puts it away. Dennis watches her do this and says nothing.

DENNIS
How long till the tide comes back?

MIRIAM
Six hours. Maybe seven.

Dennis nods. He sets his shoes on the rail, lines them up, takes them down again. Fatima tucks the coat tighter around the child's shoulders. Tomasz watches her hands.

Petronella's needles click. Jasper climbs from the hatch and stands, looking out at the sandbar, at the bright far line of the sea. He rolls his sleeves to the elbow. He rolls them down.

JASPER
Engine's fine.

MIRIAM
I know.

JASPER
It's the tide that's the problem.

MIRIAM
I know.

Fatima rises and walks to the water jug. She pours a cup, brings it back to Tomasz. He takes it. He drinks half and gives the rest to the child, who stirs and sips without waking. Dennis turns his face to the sky as if looking for rain.

DENNIS
(to the deck)
We could walk.

MIRIAM
No.

DENNIS
It's only a mile.

MIRIAM
The channel is deeper than it looks.

Dennis shrugs. He sits on the rail. Petronella's needles click twice and stop. She holds up her knitting to the light and examines it, turns it over, resumes.

TOMASZ
Is there more water?

JASPER
Two jugs.

TOMASZ
Then there is enough.

Nobody argues. Gulls cry overhead. Out on the flat, a line of ripples begins to form, very slowly, a long way off. Miriam sees it first. She keeps her face still. She picks up her cap and puts it on. She says nothing to anyone, and one by one the others notice her, and notice what she is looking at, and sit very straight and quiet to wait.

FATIMA
Is that the tide?

MIRIAM
That is the tide.

Fatima touches the child's hair. Tomasz closes his eyes. Dennis sets his shoes down, side by side, with care. Jasper wipes his hands on the rag one more time, unhurried, and folds it, and puts it in his pocket.
